{"capture_id":"kco-202609-fdd587d3bdcbf08d4b2a61beb0c8a5b3aafcd18f2d2eba99015208dc6f5a1da3","captured_at":"2026-09-03T18:38:33Z","event_type":"observation.captured","partition":"observations/pattern/2026-09.jsonl","request":{"competency_facets":["CQ-ORIENT"],"contract_version":"knowledge-captured-observation.v1","destination_hint":{"path":"docs/knowledge/topics","type":"topic"},"freshness_anchor":{"digest":{"byte_length":18668,"kind":"sha256-bytes-v1","sha256":"0b01694ab850096c3c44f0f562f95408c53fc787310158ec921f780077124935"},"path":"docs/specs/shaping-review-contracts/spec.md"},"kind":"pattern","lesson":"The 150-word acceptance-criterion cap this topic recommends is refused by this repository. docs/specs/shaping-review-contracts/spec.md ships the ticked criterion \"new-spec and shaping review reject hard AC word budgets\", new-spec/SKILL.md states that shaping review \"additionally rejects hard AC word budgets\", and RFC-0099 records that no hard word budget is added. The underlying signal is real but is a sampler, not a bound: criterion length orders criteria for the semantic-atomicity test, longest first, and the conjunction and substitution rules in the spec template decide whether a criterion splits. Measured across 6,586 criteria under docs/specs on 2026-09-03, the median is 35 words, p90 is 102, and p95 is 149, so a 150-word cap would sit at the existing p95 and still not be a contract anyone agreed to.","observed_at":"2026-09-03T14:00:00Z","privacy_attestation":{"contains_instructions":false,"contains_private_data":false,"contains_secrets":false,"reviewed":true},"producer":{"workflow":"project-knowledge","workflow_version":"direct-maintainer"},"project_scope":{"audience":"project","paths":["docs/specs","packs/core/.apm/skills/new-spec"]},"provenance":{"sources":[{"path":"docs/specs/shaping-review-contracts/spec.md"}]},"semantic_gate":{"artifact":"docs/specs/shaping-review-contracts/spec.md","name":"spec-approved"}},"request_sha256":"fdd587d3bdcbf08d4b2a61be